From: pdx203 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: frontend: 64-byte IOSurface row alignment; render pass profile behind a switch

QuartzCore sizes its IOSurfaces' rows by the device's iosurfaceReadOnlyTextureAlignmentBytes, which
answered the runtime's 16-byte linear alignment: the render server's 1096-pixel surfaces had
4384-byte rows. No linear image aliases such a texture on the Adreno (64-byte pitches), so each was a
shadow image copied in for every command buffer, and Turnip split the copy into one 2D blit per row
(4384 is not 64-byte aligned): ~13000 row blits and ~9 ms of GPU per home-screen frame. It now
answers 64 (iosurfaceTextureAlignmentBytes stays unanswered). RhizomePoster then crosses its 200 MB
memory high watermark once at boot (an EXC_RESOURCE report; it keeps running).

Measured on pdx203 (home-screen paging): frame command buffer GPU 12.5 -> 3.3 ms,
submission retire latency 13.4 -> 3.5 ms, median swap gap 29 -> 17 ms, GPU busy 28 -> 10 %.

The profile that found it stays, off unless /private/var/ngpu/debug/pass-profile exists: Turnip's
pass stamps in backboardd (SHENG_DIAG for Turnip and the runtime only), NBPassPoll's PDX203PASS /
PASSTOP / CB / TL / COPY / BLIT rows through os_log. NBGpuTimes and the blit counters are always on.

